WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:04.820
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.120
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:07.400
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:08.660
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.480
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:19.460
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:20.600
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:25.210
Hello.

9
00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:26.170
Hey, lady.

10
00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:27.850
I'm excited today.

11
00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:29.390
I am so excited.

12
00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:33.310
We get to share our interview with a very special gueSt.

13
00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:35.490
Bob Odenkirk.

14
00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:36.410
Yes.

15
00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:39.290
He comes in the studio with us.

16
00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:40.930
For a very long chat.

17
00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:41.630
I know.

18
00:00:41.890 --> 00:00:48.170
Well, you know, Bob is probably best known for playing lawyer Saul Goodman on Breaking Bad and Better Call Saul.

19
00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:59.730
And in the world of The Office, he plays Mark Franks, the manager of a Philadelphia real estate company that Pam has an interview with during season nine, Moving On.

20
00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:01.170
We just broke down.

21
00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:01.870
Yes.

22
00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:03.530
Moving On, part one and part two.

23
00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:08.130
So we're very excited to talk to Bob about his guest star on the show.

24
00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:10.750
Bob Odenkirk is a writer.

25
00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:11.770
He is a director.

26
00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:12.830
He is an actor.

27
00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:15.530
His career, it is extensive.

28
00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:17.150
Very impressive.

29
00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:23.010
You know, at the end of our interview, Ange, Bob said something that I just, I loved it.

30
00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:25.290
He said, I love talking about showbiz.

31
00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:26.330
He does.

32
00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:34.270
He's a real, true fan of the medium, of television, of film, of sketch comedy.

33
00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:35.630
And it really comes through.

34
00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:37.910
Well, I think it really comes through in this interview.

35
00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.350
You know, he definitely talks about his time on The Office.

36
00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:43.330
But mostly, we talk about his career.

37
00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:53.690
We talk about writing on Saturday Night Live, working with Second City, Mr. Show, the Ben Stiller Show, and then also his transition from comedy into drama.

38
00:01:53.690 --> 00:02:05.170
One of the things that we talked about that I was trying so hard not to geek out on, but he wrote the Saturday Night Live sketch, The Motivational Speaker, for Chris Farley.

39
00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:05.650
Yes.

40
00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:08.150
I mean, that is like an epic sketch.

41
00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:11.590
It's the man that lives in the van down by the river.

42
00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:13.370
Oh, gosh.

43
00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:18.350
If you haven't seen it, maybe before this interview, take a moment and go watch that sketch.

44
00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:19.330
It's so great.

45
00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:24.090
And Bob tells us the background story of how that sketch came to be.

46
00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:26.470
I'm going to link to it all in our stories.

47
00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:28.810
And we'll also put a link in our show notes.

48
00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:29.310
Yes.

49
00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:33.550
So if you want to pause for a second and watch it, it's right down there in the show notes.

50
00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.790
You know, I've known Bob a very long time.

51
00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.730
His wife, Naomi, is my manager.

52
00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:40.990
She's my only manager that I've ever had.

53
00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:43.910
So I have socialized with Bob and Naomi.

54
00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:44.730
We've gone to dinner.

55
00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:48.230
But I've never, like, asked him about his career.

56
00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:49.310
Not at dinner.

57
00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:50.170
That'd be weird.

58
00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:50.690
No, no.

59
00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:57.690
You know, but this was really fun for me because I got to hear the stories I've always wanted to hear, but I was too shy to ask, basically.

60
00:02:57.970 --> 00:02:58.210
Right.

61
00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:03.330
I started watching Bob on Mr. Show, you know, ages ago.

62
00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:08.510
And as someone that loves improv and sketch comedy, he's a legend.

63
00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:08.970
Yeah.

64
00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:10.130
Jenna, you know this.

65
00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:13.210
I was trying very hard not to be a total dork in front of him.

66
00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:14.450
I don't think I succeeded.

67
00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:22.550
But it was just so amazing to get to hear all of the stories of his early days in show business and the lessons he's learned along the way.

68
00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:24.390
Well, why don't we take a break?

69
00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:28.150
And then when we come back, our interview with Bob Odenkirk.

70
00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:28.710
Yeah.

71
00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:40.260
Kirk.

72
00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:40.840
Hi.

73
00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.040
Welcome to Office Ladies.

74
00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:43.680
Thank you.

75
00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:44.700
It's good to be here.

76
00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:47.820
Great always to see you and you.

77
00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:50.200
We're older friends.

78
00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:51.180
Yes, you guys.

79
00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:52.840
You've known each other a long time.

80
00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.400
A very long time.

81
00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:54.800
Yeah.

82
00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:58.700
Bob, this was our first time acting together, was on The Office.

83
00:03:58.840 --> 00:03:59.100
Oh, yeah.

84
00:03:59.240 --> 00:03:59.420
Yeah.

85
00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:04.180
But you had directed me in two projects, Melvin Goes to Dinner and Brother Solomon.

86
00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:04.880
I love it.

87
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:06.480
Thank you for being a part of those.

88
00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:07.460
Oh, my gosh.

89
00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:11.660
That Brother Solomon script is, to this day, one of the best scripts I've ever read.

90
00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:12.920
It was written by Will Forte.

91
00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:13.200
Yeah.

92
00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.500
And it's Will Forte, Will Arnett, Kristen Wiig.

93
00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:17.100
Yeah.

94
00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:18.080
Malin Ackerman.

95
00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:18.620
Yes.

96
00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:19.260
So good.

97
00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:23.900
And, yeah, and Sean McBride, please.

98
00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:25.460
It's a great script.

99
00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:27.640
I wish I'd made a better film out of it.

100
00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:28.900
What are you talking about?

101
00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:29.920
It's so funny.

102
00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:34.600
I have an idea that I wished that I had done.

103
00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:35.880
What?

104
00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:37.780
This is a kind of big regret.

105
00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:38.500
What?

106
00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:41.560
We were not expecting to talk about this today.

107
00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.040
Let's kick it off with regrets.

108
00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:45.980
Okay, so it was such a funny script.

109
00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:46.620
Yes.

110
00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:50.500
Matt Berenson was one of the producers, and he had given it to me.

111
00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:56.100
And I wasn't quite sure what to do with it, but I loved Will Forte, and I loved Will

112
00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:56.600
Arnett.

113
00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:01.600
And I could picture it as being a really fun and sweet movie, which I needed.

114
00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:03.760
You know, sometimes you need that in your life.

115
00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:10.020
A lot of things that you make, and especially me, kind of have a harshness or an edge to

116
00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:10.680
them, you know?

117
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.820
That's just common, I think, in things.

118
00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:20.740
We like to watch things that are harsh because they hopefully aren't like your real life, and

119
00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:22.600
you get to voyeuristically.

120
00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:27.060
Also, we like to see people under pressure.

121
00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:27.760
Yes.

122
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:34.080
And pressured situations are often presented in movies and TV, right?

123
00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.580
Because you want to watch how people behave, you know?

124
00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:38.240
And you don't want to experience it yourself.

125
00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:39.660
You want to watch someone else.

126
00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:42.380
I find those movies relax me.

127
00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:42.660
Jenna?

128
00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:43.080
Oh, yeah?

129
00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:43.620
Yes.

130
00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:44.320
Uncut geMs.

131
00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:45.060
Like horror filMs.

132
00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:46.580
Oh, I love Uncut GeMs.

133
00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:46.800
Right?

134
00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.300
Some people can't watch that.

135
00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:48.540
It's too much.

136
00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:50.360
But there's a lot of things I can't watch.

137
00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:51.980
Prince of the Pauper, I can't watch.

138
00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:52.700
I don't know that.

139
00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:57.080
It's an old Mark Twain story made into a film in like the 40s or 50s.

140
00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:03.460
But anything where the thing could be solved, if somebody would just be honest in a simple

141
00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:06.220
way, drives me f***ing crazy.

142
00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:06.680
Yes!

143
00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:08.160
I'm like, just tell her!

144
00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:08.780
Yes!

145
00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:10.420
Just say I'm the prince!

146
00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:12.440
Yes, I changed clothes!

147
00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:13.940
It's obvious!

148
00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:15.040
Well, Jenna!

149
00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:24.460
You know, if the problem can be solved by simply being honest, you know, it really

150
00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:27.240
is, it drives me nuts.

151
00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:28.100
It's too frustrating for me.

152
00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:30.740
Some part of me inside is dying every second.

153
00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:32.520
That is so you, Jenna.

154
00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:33.400
It's so me.

155
00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:35.420
But Uncut Gems cannot be solved.

156
00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:36.120
No, it cannot.

157
00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:36.840
No, it cannot.

158
00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:40.640
And that guy's an addict, and he's going off the rails, and we're going to watch him

159
00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:41.020
go off the rails.

160
00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:41.520
No, I can't do it.

161
00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:42.480
It is so great.

162
00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:43.100
I love it.

163
00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:44.940
She also, I don't know.

164
00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:46.220
Also, Sandler's so good in it.

165
00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:50.560
Well, she thinks every day the, like, zombie apocalypse might happen.

166
00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:50.940
Oh, I don't know.

167
00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:52.060
So that's how she operates.

168
00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:53.860
She's got, you've got your bag ready.

169
00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:55.960
You're, like, good-to-go survivaliSt.

170
00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:56.300
Bag, Angela.

171
00:06:56.860 --> 00:06:57.200
Three bags.

172
00:06:57.320 --> 00:06:58.020
Okay, three bags.

173
00:06:58.280 --> 00:06:58.760
I'm sorry, three bags.

174
00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:02.800
And so she likes, I think, movies that are really stressful, because then she's like,

175
00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:03.520
I got this.

176
00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:04.660
Do you watch horror films?

177
00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:07.160
Yes, I'm a huge horror film lover.

178
00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:07.640
I hate them.

179
00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:08.640
I can't do it.

180
00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:09.340
You don't like a horror film?

181
00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:10.080
I can't watch them, no.

182
00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:10.480
Really?

183
00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:11.620
No, I don't want to.

184
00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:13.120
You have two choices.

185
00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:19.220
You can let yourself be sucked into the invented reality and get scared.

186
00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:19.700
Yeah.

187
00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:21.440
Which most people do.

188
00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:21.920
Me.

189
00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:26.820
Or you can sort of mentally make an effort to remove yourself and say, I am in a theater

190
00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:27.380
right now.

191
00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:28.740
This is not real.

192
00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:29.220
Mm-hmm.

193
00:07:29.380 --> 00:07:31.660
And then you're not getting the experience.

194
00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:37.620
Why are you there if all you're going to do is remove yourself from it because you don't

195
00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:38.700
want to get all scared?

196
00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:39.200
Right.

197
00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:43.240
And so I don't see the value of either of those.

198
00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:44.420
I don't want to be scared.

199
00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:49.080
And I don't want to sit somewhere and go, this isn't real.

200
00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:49.920
This isn't real.

201
00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:50.820
This isn't real.

202
00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:52.200
It's a very practical way.

203
00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:54.840
Like, what is, what is, there's no in between.

204
00:07:55.460 --> 00:07:56.500
So I don't get it.

205
00:07:56.620 --> 00:07:57.180
I don't get it.

206
00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:03.300
I am, in a strange way, a very earnest person for a person in comedy, you know?

207
00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:03.680
Mm-hmm.

208
00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:12.600
I am oddly earnest, and I think it's one of the things that makes me a little, it's

209
00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:18.780
one of the reasons I think Steve Carell is a better, you know, is the one who got the

210
00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:19.020
role.

211
00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:19.900
It was me and Steve.

212
00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:20.600
I know.

213
00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:20.900
Yeah.

214
00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:21.680
That's right.

215
00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:27.060
But I really think when it comes to this, and there were other parts that I think I

216
00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:30.100
was up for that Steve got.

217
00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:35.240
And it's because he's better at being genuinely fun.

218
00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:36.560
Uh-huh.

219
00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:37.740
Genuinely fun.

220
00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:44.540
And I think I bring with me a little bit too much earnest seriousness.

221
00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:46.840
And it's just kind of there.

222
00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:53.500
And there's nothing I can do about it except play other roles where it's helpful to have

223
00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:53.740
that.

224
00:08:53.740 --> 00:09:00.740
And, you know, you just don't believe me as a purely light character.

225
00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:01.420
You don't.

226
00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:02.040
Mm-hmm.

227
00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:04.500
You just are looking for the darkness.

228
00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:08.120
And that's actually great in drama.

229
00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:08.700
Yeah.

230
00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:10.700
That's a plus, you know?

231
00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:12.460
But in comedy, it's not a plus.

232
00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:14.060
I remember being on stage.

233
00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:17.880
I've told this story before, and it's a very minor story.

234
00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:23.940
But I really think that at the core of it is this thing I'm talking about, which is I was

235
00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:27.140
on stage with Jill Talley and Chris Farley at Second City.

236
00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:27.720
Oh, man.

237
00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:28.760
And we were improvising.

238
00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:31.180
And I look at the audience.

239
00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:32.020
They're right here.

240
00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:33.260
And they're laughing.

241
00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:41.380
And I'm thinking, if I was sitting there, I would be watching Chris Farley or Jill Talley.

242
00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:44.200
And the last person I'd be watching is Bob Odenkirk.

243
00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:50.820
And this is all during a scene, but, you know, when you do a show seven times a week

244
00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:56.220
at Second City, your brain can really, like, you can obviously do your scripted stuff and

245
00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:58.940
be thinking about groceries and anything.

246
00:09:59.180 --> 00:09:59.480
Yeah.

247
00:09:59.760 --> 00:09:59.960
Yeah.

248
00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:05.320
And you can even improvise and have your brain go, what is tomorrow morning?

249
00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:07.000
I got to get up early tomorrow morning.

250
00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:16.620
And I thought, boy, but if this was a drama, I think maybe I'd hold my own up here.

251
00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:21.620
Because there's a quality of comedy performers of many of them.

252
00:10:21.780 --> 00:10:26.320
This is, there's no across the board hard and fast rules in this kind of theorizing.

253
00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:33.240
But where you can, like, relax if you know who they are, like, right away.

254
00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:33.600
Yeah.

255
00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:36.100
Off their face, their smile, their energy.

256
00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:44.260
And anything that's murky is, like, makes it harder to enjoy the light humor that's happening.

257
00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:48.500
And I think that's where I really struggle.

258
00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:51.480
And I've done plenty of sketch comedy and stuff.

259
00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:58.180
But I think I've never been the outstanding performer until I was able to do drama.

260
00:10:58.460 --> 00:11:03.220
Then I was able to really kind of be 100% there and helpful.

261
00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:07.580
In comedy, you know, I'm not bringing that light energy.

262
00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:13.180
I mean, I always point to Will Ferrell as, like, he is the epitome to me of, like,

263
00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:15.920
I'm smiling when he walks.

264
00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:16.860
Yes.

265
00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:17.320
Yes.

266
00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:20.100
If he's on screen, you gotta smile.

267
00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:23.380
He can be doing the craziest, dumbest thing, but.

268
00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:25.600
Or, like, just the most mundane thing.

269
00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:25.800
Yes.

270
00:11:25.800 --> 00:11:26.480
And you're smiling.

271
00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:26.860
Yes.

272
00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:26.980
Yeah.

273
00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:27.460
That's right.

274
00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:30.420
He's just walked into the shoe store and picked up a pair of shoes.

275
00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:32.480
And he's wondering if he's gonna buy them.

276
00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:32.650
Yeah.

277
00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:33.680
And you're, like, on the floor.

278
00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:34.060
Right.

279
00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:34.320
Yeah.

280
00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:35.120
Why is that?

281
00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:36.820
It's just a thing.

282
00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:37.700
It's an ineffable.

283
00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:38.880
It's his energy thing.

284
00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:39.640
Except is it effable?

285
00:11:39.860 --> 00:11:41.900
Because am I not effing it right now?

286
00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:43.780
Am I not pointing to it right now?

287
00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:47.100
It is a mirthful aura.

288
00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:48.480
Yeah.

289
00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:52.480
Well, Bob, you mentioned that you auditioned for Michael Scott.

290
00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:53.020
Yeah.

291
00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:53.460
Right?

292
00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:54.220
Yes, I did.

293
00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:59.080
And I know I've told this story before on the podcast, but, Bob, you and I prepared for

294
00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:03.280
our test audition together because your wife, Naomi Odenkirk, is my manager.

295
00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:09.220
And when I was asked to test for the role of Pam, she said, oh, Bob is testing for the

296
00:12:09.220 --> 00:12:10.100
role of Michael Scott.

297
00:12:10.340 --> 00:12:11.380
You two should get together.

298
00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:14.400
And I didn't have a lot of improv experience.

299
00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:18.460
So it was just so great to get to prepare with you in that way.

300
00:12:18.460 --> 00:12:26.080
And I remember that test and it really came down to you and Steve Carell.

301
00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:30.780
You know, I mean, I think the office was exactly what it needed to be.

302
00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:35.840
What you guys did and Steve did was exactly what it needed to be.

303
00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:36.860
And it was great.

304
00:12:36.860 --> 00:12:38.500
So it's interesting.

305
00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:43.620
I mean, I, you know, I think I, I've been in the business, even at that point, I've been

306
00:12:43.620 --> 00:12:50.880
in it so long that I don't, I didn't audition for much in my career.

307
00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:56.320
Um, when I was younger, I did auditions because they called me and said, you've got an audition.

308
00:12:56.320 --> 00:12:59.180
And I said, oh, I guess I go do it.

309
00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:04.480
But there came a point where I, I probably wasn't getting them, even though I was getting

310
00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:08.400
roles, but, and I just never asked for them.

311
00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:11.240
I never called my agent and said, where are my auditions?

312
00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:13.200
I was just writing.

313
00:13:13.420 --> 00:13:17.400
I just woke up and wrote, you know, and yeah, I was thankful to audition.

314
00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:22.300
I had a great time auditioning, uh, and I love Greg Daniels.

315
00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:29.100
I mean, we knew each other for a long time and I love the office, the BBC version.

316
00:13:29.100 --> 00:13:30.300
I love very much.

317
00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:34.600
So I, oh, a big part of it for me was Curb.

318
00:13:35.340 --> 00:13:40.980
And of course in Curb, you're improvising off, um, you know, an outline less than you

319
00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:46.080
guys had a great deal less, I would say, but you guys still had room to improvise.

320
00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:46.320
Right.

321
00:13:46.400 --> 00:13:50.200
So I knew, oh, there's going to be, this is like Curb.

322
00:13:50.260 --> 00:13:52.760
And I had had such a good time on Curb.

323
00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:54.860
It was doing that show.

324
00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:59.120
I was like, oh my God, this is the best thing you can get in show business.

325
00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:05.120
If you can get this to happen, a show with an ensemble of people that you like and that

326
00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:11.720
are funny and that you get room to just invent and just goof around, this is the beSt.

327
00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:18.200
So I was excited to do the auditions for the office because I knew it had that dimension.

328
00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:24.120
And look, when I did the Ben Stiller show, I walked away from that going, I got to create

329
00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:26.060
a show again with my friends.

330
00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:26.960
Yeah.

331
00:14:27.340 --> 00:14:31.580
Waking up in the morning and picking up Andy Dick and picking up Ginny Garofalo and driving

332
00:14:31.580 --> 00:14:38.780
out to wherever the hell we had some cheap ass set or we're stealing locations and goofing

333
00:14:38.780 --> 00:14:42.700
around all day long and laughing is the greatest, you know?

334
00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:47.000
So when that show ended, I was like, I have to recreate that.

335
00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:50.540
And so when I finished Curb, I was like, I have to recreate that.

336
00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:52.020
But I still haven't.

337
00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:54.540
Are you still working on it?

338
00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:55.820
Not really.

339
00:14:56.180 --> 00:15:03.020
This weird thing happened where I got into a drama and it's a lot of work and you don't

340
00:15:03.020 --> 00:15:04.300
improvise at all.

341
00:15:04.780 --> 00:15:10.620
And then I got into action movies, which again, you don't, you know, it's, you can, you know,

342
00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:16.140
obviously like any movie, but even maybe more, maybe it's more malleable than a lot of

343
00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:20.080
films, you know, you can work with the moment and invent.

344
00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:23.260
And that is especially true of the fight sequences.

345
00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:24.280
Really?

346
00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:24.800
Interesting.

347
00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:25.200
Yes.

348
00:15:25.460 --> 00:15:26.660
Wait, they're not just like.

349
00:15:26.680 --> 00:15:27.780
They are choreographed.

350
00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:29.700
They're absolutely choreographed and rehearsed.

351
00:15:29.820 --> 00:15:35.980
But when you get on set and it's time to do it and you see a shot you didn't have, you

352
00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:43.640
see a move that you didn't think of, the set isn't quite built the way the layout was and

353
00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:49.800
something goes wrong or looks better and you go, no, no, no, no, no, don't hit me.

354
00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:50.640
I'll hit him.

355
00:15:51.480 --> 00:15:54.280
If you fall over and I'll punch you in the face.

356
00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:56.940
And then everybody goes, ah, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

357
00:15:57.140 --> 00:16:01.860
And then you do that thing and then you look at it on camera and you go, oh, that was awesome.

358
00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:03.300
That looks so real.

359
00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:10.400
Well, I was very surprised to discover that aspect of doing that, of the inventiveness

360
00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:14.820
and teamwork that those sequences, I think, always take.

361
00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:17.040
But I mean, every movie's different.

362
00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:23.200
But I'm going to keep those in my repertoire, I hope, if I make more action movies, which

363
00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:24.200
it looks like I might.

364
00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:27.600
Well, shocker, we have never done an action movie, Jenna and I.

365
00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:28.260
Does that surprise you?

366
00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:28.660
Does that surprise you?

367
00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:29.360
That we are not.

368
00:16:29.380 --> 00:16:31.720
Well, doesn't it surprise you that I did be one?

369
00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:37.140
I mean, in a way, I remember when Naomi told me that you were going to do your first action

370
00:16:37.140 --> 00:16:39.080
film and I was like, really?

371
00:16:40.120 --> 00:16:40.520
Really?

372
00:16:40.800 --> 00:16:42.520
Jenna, we shouldn't count ourselves out.

373
00:16:42.980 --> 00:16:44.700
Maybe we should out there for us.

374
00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:45.220
Absolutely.

375
00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:46.220
Mom Detectives.

376
00:16:47.380 --> 00:16:47.780
Action.

377
00:16:48.220 --> 00:16:49.540
That's our spinoff show.

378
00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:50.100
Detectives.

379
00:16:50.200 --> 00:16:51.160
Yeah, Mom Detectives.

380
00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:52.400
Just don't punch anything.

381
00:16:52.540 --> 00:16:53.180
We do.

382
00:16:53.420 --> 00:16:54.300
Mom Detectives do.

383
00:16:55.020 --> 00:16:56.840
Solving crimes no one cares about.

384
00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:57.960
That's our log line.

385
00:16:58.420 --> 00:17:01.700
I did an improv pilot right before I got The Office.

386
00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:02.300
Oh, wow.

387
00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:04.340
It was called Salon Royale.

388
00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:08.820
And it was me and Ian Roberts and Betty Cahill and Dave Brzezowski.

389
00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:09.220
Wow.

390
00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:11.660
And it was for the Oxygen Network.

391
00:17:11.820 --> 00:17:15.580
It was the most fun I had had, like, ever on a job.

392
00:17:15.580 --> 00:17:16.860
And I just knew it was going to go.

393
00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:18.480
It was just amazing.

394
00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:20.600
And then it didn't go.

395
00:17:21.160 --> 00:17:24.220
But if it had gone, I wouldn't have gotten The Office.

396
00:17:24.460 --> 00:17:26.000
And I always think about that.

397
00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:33.080
But that kind of chemistry of having this loose outline and getting to improvise the beats of a scene with people who you truSt.

398
00:17:33.260 --> 00:17:35.080
And it's so much fun.

399
00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:35.920
Oh, it's the beSt.

400
00:17:36.180 --> 00:17:36.460
Yeah.

401
00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:43.920
I mean, I think acting in a drama that's really well written and intense and, you know, layered and all that.

402
00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:49.240
But, you know, Better Call Saul, the star of the show is the writing, you know.

403
00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:56.900
And that has a great reward when you finish a scene and it's difficult and you feel that you connected with it, honestly.

404
00:17:57.560 --> 00:18:03.180
But it's not as much fun as doing this, what we're talking about.

405
00:18:03.700 --> 00:18:03.780
Yeah.

406
00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:08.940
And, look, I would love, Jenna, I would love to find a show where I got to do this.

407
00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:10.360
It really is the most fun.

408
00:18:10.500 --> 00:18:11.760
Because I am a writer, too.

409
00:18:12.040 --> 00:18:15.900
So, it lets you use that part of you or you're inventing things.

410
00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:17.380
And there's no question.

411
00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:20.760
You know, you do this thing and they say cut and you all laugh.

412
00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:23.500
And that's just a good sign.

413
00:18:23.620 --> 00:18:24.320
It's the best time.

414
00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:25.540
If you're laughing at work.

415
00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:27.000
We laughed every day.

416
00:18:27.040 --> 00:18:28.580
We did on the set of The Office.

417
00:18:28.780 --> 00:18:31.600
Like, every day there was a moment where you ruined a take because you were laughing.

418
00:18:31.960 --> 00:18:34.720
Bob, you mentioned that you are also a writer.

419
00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:41.320
And we wanted to read you this fan question from John Grant in Raleigh, North Carolina, because he's going to be really happy today.

420
00:18:42.360 --> 00:18:46.320
John said, I am the world's biggest Bob Odenkirk fan.

421
00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:50.920
I became a huge fan of The Ben Stiller Show through to Mr. Show.

422
00:18:51.280 --> 00:18:54.180
Bumping into him on our honeymoon was unbelievable.

423
00:18:54.540 --> 00:18:57.760
So, you have met John Grant from Raleigh, North Carolina.

424
00:18:58.500 --> 00:19:08.860
He went on to say, while he's often known for Saul Goodman, he is a comedy legend responsible for Chris Farley's Matt Foley Van Down by the River sketch.

425
00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:12.800
And Mr. Show was groundbreaking and legendary in comedy.

426
00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:15.340
Can you please have him on the podcast?

427
00:19:16.180 --> 00:19:19.800
Yes, John, we are having him on the podcast right now.

428
00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:20.050
Hi, John.

429
00:19:20.180 --> 00:19:20.780
How are you?

430
00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:26.160
I love that you were responsible for the Matt Foley Van Down by the River sketch.

431
00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:27.500
That's right.

432
00:19:27.500 --> 00:19:28.120
I am.

433
00:19:28.260 --> 00:19:29.140
I wrote that sketch.

434
00:19:29.380 --> 00:19:30.040
You wrote that for Chris.

435
00:19:30.040 --> 00:19:30.840
All by myself.

436
00:19:31.460 --> 00:19:32.600
That is bonkers.

437
00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:35.560
I will never forget writing that sketch.

438
00:19:36.940 --> 00:19:38.160
How did it come about?

439
00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:47.280
It was actually this weird, my whole time at SNL, and this is part of it, because I wrote it at Second City.

440
00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:50.020
I was supposed to be at SNL.

441
00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:56.340
Literally, when I wrote it, I was, I've told this story in my book.

442
00:19:56.340 --> 00:20:05.860
In my third season of SNL, my friend Tom Giannis got hired to direct the main stage at Second City.

443
00:20:06.160 --> 00:20:14.240
I grew up in Chicago, and I went to Second City when I was 14, and it was a guiding light, you know, in that town of theater and comedy.

444
00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:21.500
And when I went to Chicago to do comedy, I very quickly sized it up and said, I am not waiting in that line.

445
00:20:22.120 --> 00:20:27.580
It's take classes and do the tour company, and no, I'm not doing it.

446
00:20:28.180 --> 00:20:29.580
I'm going to write my own stuff.

447
00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:31.240
I'm going to sink or swim.

448
00:20:31.700 --> 00:20:33.600
But nothing against Second City.

449
00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:34.280
It's great.

450
00:20:34.480 --> 00:20:40.820
It's just, I don't want to, that's not why I'm in show business, you know.

451
00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:46.600
So, anyhow, I got invited to do the main stage of Second City.

452
00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:50.200
I was my third year at Saturday Night Live.

453
00:20:50.580 --> 00:20:52.740
I had such a hard time at SNL.

454
00:20:52.780 --> 00:20:54.480
I was not very helpful there.

455
00:20:55.860 --> 00:20:58.500
I did help Robert Smigel with a lot of sketches.

456
00:20:58.500 --> 00:21:03.420
I wrote jokes and pitched things, and he used a lot of things that I gave him.

457
00:21:03.560 --> 00:21:05.940
But truthfully, I just struggled.

458
00:21:05.940 --> 00:21:13.760
And then my friend Tom calls me up, and rehearsals begin in, like, February or March for their next review.

459
00:21:14.440 --> 00:21:19.600
And SNL, in the last three months of its season, has, like, two weeks on, two weeks off.

460
00:21:20.200 --> 00:21:21.760
Well, first of all, I grew up in Chicago.

461
00:21:21.760 --> 00:21:22.840
I got to say yes.

462
00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:28.020
If you invite me to pitch for the Cubs, I'm going to say, I'm going to suck, but I'm going to say yes.

463
00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:28.280
Yeah.

464
00:21:28.280 --> 00:21:36.100
You know, and you invite me to do Second City, I'm going to suck, but I am going to take you up on that.

465
00:21:36.940 --> 00:21:38.000
So I said yes.

466
00:21:38.500 --> 00:21:47.720
And then I would go to Chicago for my two-week breaks every month, plenty of time to write and rehearse and be with the cast and plan.

467
00:21:48.060 --> 00:21:52.680
But then on those weeks where we had shows, they still needed me to be coming in.

468
00:21:52.680 --> 00:21:54.000
We were developing a show.

469
00:21:54.020 --> 00:22:03.800
So I would work at SNL Monday night, Tuesday night, all night, Wednesday read-through in the afternoon, Wednesday after read-through, fly to Chicago.

470
00:22:03.920 --> 00:22:04.420
Oh, my gosh.

471
00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:05.580
Do the improv set.

472
00:22:06.260 --> 00:22:11.320
And, you know, then stay Thursday, stay Friday, fly back to New York on Saturday morning.

473
00:22:11.500 --> 00:22:18.640
And Lauren doesn't even know I left because Conan and Robert and everybody pretended I was still there just around the corner somewhere.

474
00:22:19.920 --> 00:22:24.480
But I wasn't particularly helpful, so I wasn't missed, you know?

475
00:22:24.800 --> 00:22:29.900
And it was really only for, like, three months, so I probably did that three times, right?

476
00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:40.020
Anyway, I did it, and it was extremely stressful, and I loved Chris, and Chris was great to me and loved me, and I loved Timmy Meadows.

477
00:22:40.020 --> 00:22:49.680
But there was tension in the world there, my little world there, because here I was, a guy who had left and was, at that moment, a writer on Saturday Night Live,

478
00:22:49.940 --> 00:23:00.860
coming back and bumping somebody who'd been standing in that line for eight years to get that part, you know, to get that slot on the main stage show.

479
00:23:01.400 --> 00:23:10.200
And I don't know why it didn't occur to me when I said I'll do it, until I'd come to Chicago and done, you know, two weeks there.

480
00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:11.940
And why does everybody hate me?

481
00:23:12.020 --> 00:23:14.720
I mean, hate me, not everybody, but a lot of people.

482
00:23:14.860 --> 00:23:17.740
I mean, literally look me in the eye like, f*** you.

483
00:23:17.960 --> 00:23:19.560
I mean, people said s*** to me.

484
00:23:19.600 --> 00:23:20.120
They did.

485
00:23:21.180 --> 00:23:22.160
They said stuff.

486
00:23:23.220 --> 00:23:25.740
People did stuff, physical stuff.

487
00:23:25.980 --> 00:23:26.280
What?

488
00:23:26.580 --> 00:23:26.820
Yes.

489
00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:29.120
Like what, they hazed you somehow?

490
00:23:29.120 --> 00:23:31.080
The lights would go out, and I would get pushed.

491
00:23:31.700 --> 00:23:33.380
Oh, my gosh.

492
00:23:34.360 --> 00:23:36.160
Comedy is no joke.

493
00:23:36.400 --> 00:23:38.100
Well, and I—

494
00:23:38.100 --> 00:23:39.740
Don't jump the line in comedy.

495
00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:44.920
But I've got to say, I remember going home and going, well, I'd be pissed, too, at me.

496
00:23:45.060 --> 00:23:46.420
I don't need this job.

497
00:23:46.540 --> 00:23:48.080
Why did I say yes to this?

498
00:23:48.080 --> 00:23:51.440
Just because I want to, because I grew up in Chicago, that's not right.

499
00:23:51.880 --> 00:23:55.640
Those people who did say I'll wait in line should get this part.

500
00:23:56.160 --> 00:23:58.860
It was wrong of me to say I would do that.

501
00:23:59.120 --> 00:24:00.860
But I mean, what do you do?

502
00:24:00.920 --> 00:24:03.580
You get overwhelmed in the moment, and you—

503
00:24:03.580 --> 00:24:03.820
Yeah.

504
00:24:04.080 --> 00:24:05.440
And again, you grew up in Chicago.

505
00:24:05.640 --> 00:24:06.980
Second City matters a lot.

506
00:24:07.080 --> 00:24:07.600
That's right.

507
00:24:07.800 --> 00:24:09.240
And it mattered a lot to me.

508
00:24:09.400 --> 00:24:10.680
I love Second City.

509
00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:13.880
I just didn't want to wait in line, but I loved it, you know?

510
00:24:14.380 --> 00:24:16.760
Anyhow, in the end, it worked out all right.

511
00:24:17.020 --> 00:24:21.480
I wrote a number of sketches for the main stage show that we did do.

512
00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:23.280
It did well, got good reviews.

513
00:24:23.280 --> 00:24:25.540
And one of those was Motivational Speaker.

514
00:24:25.760 --> 00:24:27.820
And I wrote that after Chris improvised.

515
00:24:27.960 --> 00:24:35.820
We did an improv one night where we were playing faculty at a school, doing an anti-drug whatever thing.

516
00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:37.200
Like, you know—

517
00:24:38.920 --> 00:24:41.380
And Chris did a coach, you know?

518
00:24:41.500 --> 00:24:44.480
He did his coach character, which is essentially Matt Foley.

519
00:24:45.220 --> 00:24:50.020
And also, I was listening, I think, to Tony Robbins at the time, like cassettes.

520
00:24:50.640 --> 00:24:54.000
And so, Motivational Speakers were kind of a new thing.

521
00:24:54.080 --> 00:25:02.540
And I thought, you know, what about this Motivational Speaker who uses himself as the negative example?

522
00:25:02.540 --> 00:25:12.000
Um, it's something stuck in me that that's funny that he's talking about himself, like, what a piece of s**t I was.

523
00:25:12.800 --> 00:25:14.360
You don't want to be like me.

524
00:25:15.020 --> 00:25:15.540
I'm like—

525
00:25:15.540 --> 00:25:17.220
Live in a van down by the river.

526
00:25:17.380 --> 00:25:25.980
And so, I was really lonely because I was—felt out of place at SNL, which mattered so much to me.

527
00:25:26.340 --> 00:25:30.600
I felt out of place at Second City, which mattered so much to me.

528
00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:35.740
I knew I'd alienated some people who were good people, who I shouldn't have done that.

529
00:25:36.160 --> 00:25:40.160
But, you know, I'm working off fumes, you know, and I love comedy.

530
00:25:40.820 --> 00:25:48.240
And I sat there in my—and not many things come out the first time you write them the way they're done.

531
00:25:48.660 --> 00:25:53.560
And that sketch I wrote right out on a legal pad that night alone in my apartment.

532
00:25:53.560 --> 00:25:57.800
And it was essentially exactly what we all did.

533
00:25:58.300 --> 00:26:05.820
And because we then did it at Second City for the next few months, it didn't play on SNL for about a year and a half, two years.

534
00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:08.600
Chris really wanted to do it at SNL.

535
00:26:08.700 --> 00:26:13.360
But I think Lauren had a little bit of a, you did that in theater, we don't need that here.

536
00:26:14.060 --> 00:26:16.600
Which is totally—I get that.

537
00:26:17.120 --> 00:26:20.780
Eventually, he relented, and it did very well on SNL.

538
00:26:21.340 --> 00:26:32.280
But anyway, I'm very—you know, I feel very lucky that that all came to me in that moment and that it played so well.

539
00:26:32.280 --> 00:26:39.060
And my daughter once asked me when she was six, what's the most fun you ever had doing your job?

540
00:26:39.600 --> 00:26:48.780
And I, without hesitation, said, every night at Second City Theater, the motivational speaker, I played the dad.

541
00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:57.460
And the one of—you know, besides for Chris just being so fun every time he did it, he made it a point to make everyone laugh.

542
00:26:57.640 --> 00:26:59.520
He was not going to leave the stage.

543
00:27:00.100 --> 00:27:02.020
By the way, every night.

544
00:27:02.420 --> 00:27:03.300
Come on.

545
00:27:03.620 --> 00:27:05.920
And he did it.

546
00:27:06.060 --> 00:27:06.820
He did it.

547
00:27:06.880 --> 00:27:08.580
And it was crazy.

548
00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:09.480
It was crazy.

549
00:27:09.820 --> 00:27:15.900
It was the greatest, greatest vibe ever to be on stage doing that.

550
00:27:16.060 --> 00:27:17.700
And also, the audience.

551
00:27:17.960 --> 00:27:19.300
Look, he wasn't famous.

552
00:27:19.300 --> 00:27:22.480
He was just some guy in a show that they paid to see.

553
00:27:23.320 --> 00:27:30.720
And—but to watch that audience transform in four minutes from people who are like, who are these young actors?

554
00:27:30.920 --> 00:27:31.660
They're kind of funny.

555
00:27:31.840 --> 00:27:33.100
As I could say, he's fun, too.

556
00:27:33.680 --> 00:27:35.080
What's that guy's name?

557
00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:36.440
Chris Farley.

558
00:27:36.660 --> 00:27:38.360
I'm going to love him forever.

559
00:27:39.040 --> 00:27:39.440
Yeah.

560
00:27:39.660 --> 00:27:40.540
In four minutes.

561
00:27:41.060 --> 00:27:41.980
Was nuts.

562
00:27:42.320 --> 00:27:43.560
And you felt it.

563
00:27:43.720 --> 00:27:44.620
You felt it.

564
00:27:44.700 --> 00:27:46.820
And I've heard about that kind of magical stuff.

565
00:27:46.820 --> 00:27:50.580
We've all heard about this person came out and they sang one song.

566
00:27:50.700 --> 00:27:54.900
And you kind of don't believe it, you know, because it doesn't happen that often.

567
00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:59.020
I mean, most things are hard-earned, hard-won, and just incremental.

568
00:27:59.660 --> 00:28:04.340
But I'm telling you, at the end of that sketch, that was it.

569
00:28:04.560 --> 00:28:04.760
Yeah.

570
00:28:04.760 --> 00:28:08.860
350 more people were like, I love Chris Farley forever.

571
00:28:11.180 --> 00:28:12.160
It was cool.

572
00:28:12.420 --> 00:28:18.920
My favorite Mr. Show sketch, which we just showed our kids recently and they are now obsessed with, is the audition.

573
00:28:19.180 --> 00:28:19.600
Oh, yeah.

574
00:28:19.780 --> 00:28:21.320
Well, I didn't write that at all.

575
00:28:21.460 --> 00:28:23.020
That was Dino Stamatopoulos.

576
00:28:23.180 --> 00:28:24.060
Oh, my gosh.

577
00:28:24.160 --> 00:28:24.380
Great.

578
00:28:24.680 --> 00:28:25.860
That is so funny.

579
00:28:25.900 --> 00:28:26.700
It's just great.

580
00:28:27.080 --> 00:28:28.200
It's too much.

581
00:28:28.720 --> 00:28:34.440
And also, the hiker who went to Mount Everest but can't finish his story because he keeps falling into the thing.

582
00:28:34.760 --> 00:28:35.680
Yes, yes, yes, yes.

583
00:28:35.880 --> 00:28:37.100
I did write that.

584
00:28:37.300 --> 00:28:37.780
You did?

585
00:28:38.020 --> 00:28:38.340
I did.

586
00:28:38.440 --> 00:28:41.100
Okay, that was the other one we showed our kids.

587
00:28:41.280 --> 00:28:43.420
Yeah, it's called The Story of EvereSt.

588
00:28:43.740 --> 00:28:45.800
Yes, and he can't ever finish it.

589
00:28:45.920 --> 00:28:53.340
It was based on, Jay told us that we were sitting in the writer's room and he talked about how when he grew up he had a grill.

590
00:28:53.860 --> 00:28:55.420
You know, one of those Weber grills?

591
00:28:55.460 --> 00:28:55.720
Sure.

592
00:28:55.880 --> 00:29:00.040
And it was in Chicago and they kept it inside the kitchen.

593
00:29:00.540 --> 00:29:01.700
You know, it's filled with holes.

594
00:29:01.700 --> 00:29:04.340
That seems like a really dangerous idea.

595
00:29:04.340 --> 00:29:06.020
It was during the winter or something.

596
00:29:06.180 --> 00:29:06.280
Cold, okay, yeah.

597
00:29:06.440 --> 00:29:08.120
But they didn't grill on it in the kitchen.

598
00:29:08.120 --> 00:29:11.220
No, you have to put it outside to light it on fire.

599
00:29:11.320 --> 00:29:12.160
Okay, I want to make sure.

600
00:29:12.380 --> 00:29:13.660
Let's just say you really should.

601
00:29:13.820 --> 00:29:14.380
Recommend it.

602
00:29:16.020 --> 00:29:21.960
And one time, his uncle leaned on it and it fell over because ash went everywhere.

603
00:29:22.400 --> 00:29:24.760
They cleaned everything in the kitchen.

604
00:29:25.640 --> 00:29:28.620
Somebody came in and said, what are you guys doing in here?

605
00:29:28.620 --> 00:29:31.980
And the uncle said, they had finished cleaning.

606
00:29:32.560 --> 00:29:40.940
Oh, well, earlier I was telling a story and, you know, I leaned on the grill right here and he did it again.

607
00:29:41.120 --> 00:29:41.620
No.

608
00:29:41.620 --> 00:29:45.020
And that f***ing killed me.

609
00:29:45.220 --> 00:29:45.440
Yeah.

610
00:29:45.640 --> 00:29:46.820
That killed me.

611
00:29:46.960 --> 00:29:50.200
I said, that's the greatest thing I've ever heard.

612
00:29:50.360 --> 00:29:51.660
That's so dumb.

613
00:29:52.100 --> 00:29:54.780
And so it became thimbles.

614
00:29:55.000 --> 00:30:00.280
And a fun thing about that sketch is I love that sketch.

615
00:30:00.500 --> 00:30:01.680
I love it so much.

616
00:30:01.680 --> 00:30:06.320
Well, for them, it's like me with horror filMs.

617
00:30:06.580 --> 00:30:11.480
Like, they don't like that dumb repetition thing that can be so funny in comedy.

618
00:30:11.840 --> 00:30:13.560
But, you know, we structured it.

619
00:30:13.640 --> 00:30:14.400
It's very structured.

620
00:30:14.580 --> 00:30:14.860
Yes.

621
00:30:15.000 --> 00:30:20.040
But also, let's do one more than the structure asks for.

622
00:30:20.320 --> 00:30:20.660
Yeah.

623
00:30:20.780 --> 00:30:22.720
And just see if we get a bigger laugh.

624
00:30:22.920 --> 00:30:23.240
Yeah.

625
00:30:23.400 --> 00:30:26.120
And we did it and it got nothing.

626
00:30:26.360 --> 00:30:29.800
The audience, everything you hear on Mr. Show is live laughter, right?

627
00:30:29.880 --> 00:30:30.060
Okay.

628
00:30:30.060 --> 00:30:31.220
We didn't sweeten laughs.

629
00:30:31.680 --> 00:30:44.720
But we did take out that beat because in the dress rehearsal of show, leaned on it one more time than is in the sketch with no buildup or anything.

630
00:30:44.720 --> 00:30:47.800
Just to see if we would get another good laugh.

631
00:30:48.180 --> 00:30:50.920
People went dead and went, oh.

632
00:30:50.920 --> 00:30:51.680
You lost them.

633
00:30:51.780 --> 00:30:54.960
Because they went from thinking, this is so crazy funny.

634
00:30:55.180 --> 00:30:55.840
I can't believe.

635
00:30:56.040 --> 00:31:00.180
How many times are they going to do this to, oh, you f*** you.

636
00:31:00.180 --> 00:31:04.960
You are not going to make me sit through this how many times, you know?

637
00:31:05.120 --> 00:31:15.340
You know, my dad's favorite joke when he was alive, it would make me laugh so hard, is my dad anticipating something that's about to happen that he's already just seen.

638
00:31:15.800 --> 00:31:17.920
Like, there was nothing funnier to him.

639
00:31:17.980 --> 00:31:20.240
And you describing that just brought me right back.

640
00:31:20.400 --> 00:31:23.920
He would get so tickled that as a kid, I would just watch him.

641
00:31:23.920 --> 00:31:24.520
Yeah.

642
00:31:24.660 --> 00:31:25.860
Like, wait for that thing.

643
00:31:26.000 --> 00:31:26.840
And he would be like, ugh.

644
00:31:27.740 --> 00:31:29.140
Like, he was building up.

645
00:31:29.320 --> 00:31:29.640
Yes.

646
00:31:29.680 --> 00:31:30.000
Yes.

647
00:31:30.360 --> 00:31:31.720
And it was so fun.

648
00:31:31.860 --> 00:31:34.700
And I think I have a soft spot in my heart for those kind of jokes now.

649
00:31:34.700 --> 00:31:39.400
Because I can hear my dad waiting for it to happen and just losing his mind.

650
00:31:39.700 --> 00:31:39.840
Yeah.

651
00:31:40.040 --> 00:31:42.060
I like that kind of humor, too.

652
00:31:42.260 --> 00:31:49.920
I mean, we did a sketch called Hunger Strike that also pissed people off because of how repetitive it is in the joke.

653
00:31:50.220 --> 00:31:52.340
But, man, I love that sketch.

654
00:31:52.340 --> 00:31:56.420
That's David Cross playing a guy on a hunger strike giving a speech.

655
00:31:56.620 --> 00:31:59.640
And all he can talk about is the food he's going to eat.

656
00:31:59.680 --> 00:32:00.020
Yes.

657
00:32:00.100 --> 00:32:06.140
And he's supposed to be talking about, like, his political, you know, standard bearing.

658
00:32:06.340 --> 00:32:10.140
But he's just going, I have one message for everyone.

659
00:32:10.620 --> 00:32:13.060
Can I please have a hot dog?

660
00:32:15.660 --> 00:32:16.540
Or whatever.

661
00:32:16.740 --> 00:32:17.780
You know, you can't stop.

662
00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:21.340
Bob, we haven't even barely talked about the office.

663
00:32:21.340 --> 00:32:21.700
We should.

664
00:32:21.840 --> 00:32:22.080
We should probably.

665
00:32:22.080 --> 00:32:25.700
Yeah, I think people listening to this might like the office, the TV show.

666
00:32:25.900 --> 00:32:27.120
This was so much fun.

667
00:32:27.340 --> 00:32:29.200
Oh, by the way, I couldn't wait to do it.

668
00:32:29.240 --> 00:32:35.060
And when I was invited by Greg to come do a rendition of Michael Scott.

669
00:32:35.260 --> 00:32:35.620
Yes.

670
00:32:35.860 --> 00:32:38.100
You know, I was like, this is the beSt.

671
00:32:38.260 --> 00:32:39.280
How did you get approached?

672
00:32:39.560 --> 00:32:40.660
Did Greg just call you?

673
00:32:40.780 --> 00:32:41.000
They just called, yeah.

674
00:32:41.240 --> 00:32:42.620
And did he pitch you the idea?

675
00:32:42.760 --> 00:32:47.560
Because I remember the idea was, Pam's going to go in for an interview at this real estate office.

676
00:32:47.720 --> 00:32:47.840
Yeah.

677
00:32:47.840 --> 00:32:54.440
And they thought it would be really funny if it was like her life was going to repeat itself now in Philadelphia.

678
00:32:54.800 --> 00:32:54.920
Right.

679
00:32:54.920 --> 00:32:56.840
And her boss is basically Michael Scott.

680
00:32:57.820 --> 00:33:04.840
And then when Greg told me that he was going to call you and have you play Mark, I was like, oh, my gosh.

681
00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:07.520
This is like, this is a dream come true.

682
00:33:07.760 --> 00:33:07.900
Yeah.

683
00:33:07.900 --> 00:33:09.900
And so you were just like, yes, I'm up for it.

684
00:33:10.020 --> 00:33:10.700
Oh, yeah.

685
00:33:10.920 --> 00:33:12.720
Like I said, I wanted to do a show.

686
00:33:12.900 --> 00:33:16.680
I'll do any, almost any show like this.

687
00:33:17.000 --> 00:33:26.180
Any show that where you have the improv as part of the part, as part of acting in it is, I think, one of the great things.

688
00:33:26.360 --> 00:33:28.640
So, yes, absolutely.

689
00:33:28.640 --> 00:33:38.960
I also think, you know, the whole thing of like having stories told about it was you or Carell, you know, gives me a chance to do it and show people what I would have done.

690
00:33:39.100 --> 00:33:46.840
You know, although I do think that what I was doing was more a tribute to Steve because that's what I was supposed to be doing.

691
00:33:46.980 --> 00:33:47.120
Yes.

692
00:33:47.120 --> 00:33:54.560
You know, I was my character was meant to be very much a version of what Steve had invented when he played the character.

693
00:33:54.840 --> 00:34:03.680
Well, there was one thing that was all you and we even got a fan question about it from Ella Edwards in England and Chloe M in Grand Prairie, Alberta,

694
00:34:04.180 --> 00:34:11.820
who said Jenna mentioned that her and Bob Odenkirk practiced their audition together and that Bob brought his guitar to do a song.

695
00:34:12.120 --> 00:34:16.400
Was your audition prep the inspiration for that scene in this episode?

696
00:34:16.820 --> 00:34:17.540
It was.

697
00:34:17.720 --> 00:34:18.180
It was.

698
00:34:18.340 --> 00:34:27.320
I told Greg, I said, Greg, you have to tell Bob to bring his guitar because Bob, doing that moment with you was so funny.

699
00:34:27.420 --> 00:34:35.440
And I should say, like, I had been auditioning for The Office for like months and then Naomi was like, hey, they just called Bob and asked him to come test for the role.

700
00:34:35.560 --> 00:34:36.720
And I was like, well, right, of course.

701
00:34:37.440 --> 00:34:39.760
Bob didn't do all the crap I did.

702
00:34:40.300 --> 00:34:44.540
And she was like, do you guys want to just like do something together because it's going to be improv.

703
00:34:44.540 --> 00:34:46.760
You guys could just work on your improv part together.

704
00:34:47.680 --> 00:34:57.580
I sat down with you and you pulled out that guitar and you started singing a song that was very similar to the one in the episode because the thing that was similar was the part where you're like,

705
00:34:57.800 --> 00:35:00.140
you're singing about how I'm attractive.

706
00:35:00.640 --> 00:35:01.700
But like I'm taken.

707
00:35:02.040 --> 00:35:02.640
But that's OK.

708
00:35:02.660 --> 00:35:04.620
But that's not what this is about anyway.

709
00:35:05.120 --> 00:35:07.020
And it was so funny.

710
00:35:07.020 --> 00:35:10.160
And so I was like, Greg, you have to make him do that.

711
00:35:10.200 --> 00:35:10.840
Oh, that's great.

712
00:35:10.980 --> 00:35:11.720
In this episode.

713
00:35:11.940 --> 00:35:12.360
Oh, wow.

714
00:35:12.660 --> 00:35:16.620
And so that was you were just improv-ing that song, if I remember.

715
00:35:16.880 --> 00:35:18.160
You were just reading the resume, right?

716
00:35:18.360 --> 00:35:23.400
I'm going to guess that Greg told me to and gave me the structure of it, probably.

717
00:35:23.680 --> 00:35:27.960
I think my memory is that he was like, just here's her resume.

718
00:35:28.260 --> 00:35:28.440
Yeah.

719
00:35:28.760 --> 00:35:30.320
Sing her resume to her.

720
00:35:30.440 --> 00:35:30.680
Yes.

721
00:35:30.680 --> 00:35:32.860
And comment on it.

722
00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:42.180
Listen, guys, I don't remember all that much except that I had the greatest time and I got to go on and on and on.

723
00:35:42.440 --> 00:35:42.940
Yeah.

724
00:35:43.400 --> 00:35:49.020
Which I'm sure you guys didn't do on a regular basis because you could never have shot the whole show.

725
00:35:49.580 --> 00:35:52.460
I mean, I remember I walked around the office.

726
00:35:52.700 --> 00:35:53.140
Yeah, yeah.

727
00:35:53.400 --> 00:35:55.240
And I talked to every person.

728
00:35:55.840 --> 00:35:56.220
Yeah.

729
00:35:56.420 --> 00:35:59.880
Like, yeah, I mean, I was just burning digital space.

730
00:36:00.960 --> 00:36:02.680
It's all in the episode, though.

731
00:36:02.840 --> 00:36:03.800
Like, that was the thing.

732
00:36:04.020 --> 00:36:05.080
Nothing got cut out.

733
00:36:05.260 --> 00:36:05.500
Yeah.

734
00:36:05.720 --> 00:36:08.860
Was it a two-parter or a one-hour show or something?

735
00:36:09.060 --> 00:36:14.240
It was, I think, meant to be a one-parter, but then they made it into two parts because they loved it all so much.

736
00:36:14.480 --> 00:36:16.160
Yes, it became a two-parter.

737
00:36:16.400 --> 00:36:17.820
Look, what an honor, you know.

738
00:36:17.980 --> 00:36:19.540
I'm so glad it worked out.

739
00:36:19.720 --> 00:36:20.580
It's the beSt.

740
00:36:20.760 --> 00:36:21.320
The beSt.

741
00:36:32.140 --> 00:36:38.700
Well, it's funny, Bob, because I don't even know if I've ever told you this, but Angela and I were driving here this morning and she was like,

742
00:36:38.700 --> 00:36:40.440
well, I know Bob, but I don't know him super well.

743
00:36:40.520 --> 00:36:41.500
You've known him a long time.

744
00:36:41.760 --> 00:36:42.480
What's he like?

745
00:36:42.560 --> 00:36:45.120
And I'm like, Angela, I have known Bob so long.

746
00:36:45.200 --> 00:36:46.460
I have been to dinner with him.

747
00:36:46.820 --> 00:36:48.380
I have worked with him.

748
00:36:48.580 --> 00:36:52.920
I am so nervous around him because he is a comedy legend.

749
00:36:53.260 --> 00:37:01.760
And it's like, I just, I'm like, Ange, he's the nicest guy, but I'm telling you, I get so nervous because it was like,

750
00:37:01.760 --> 00:37:06.660
now you're doing Breaking Bad and you're doing Better Call Saul and it's like, what can he not do?

751
00:37:06.940 --> 00:37:07.480
A lot.

752
00:37:07.680 --> 00:37:08.480
What doesn't he do?

753
00:37:08.500 --> 00:37:08.740
Oh, boy.

754
00:37:08.920 --> 00:37:09.920
It's a long liSt.

755
00:37:10.040 --> 00:37:11.140
This is like, it's getting worse.

756
00:37:11.400 --> 00:37:13.780
There's an answer to that question.

757
00:37:14.180 --> 00:37:17.740
But I mean, it's just such an honor to have you here today.

758
00:37:17.760 --> 00:37:18.560
You know I can't sing.

759
00:37:18.780 --> 00:37:21.360
If you've watched my comedy, you know I can't sing.

760
00:37:21.480 --> 00:37:22.540
Well, I can't sing either.

761
00:37:22.540 --> 00:37:26.400
I love to make people listen to me sing badly.

762
00:37:26.400 --> 00:37:28.120
I really do.

763
00:37:28.460 --> 00:37:32.900
But, you know, there's really, there's two things that I can do.

764
00:37:33.060 --> 00:37:39.180
I can write sketches and I can do a certain kind of drama acting.

765
00:37:39.500 --> 00:37:42.280
And I really, and I believe I can do those things.

766
00:37:42.280 --> 00:37:48.400
You know, by the way, I am good with people owning what they're good at.

767
00:37:48.540 --> 00:37:53.640
Or, you know, we are in showbiz and we're constantly meeting people who are great at things.

768
00:37:53.640 --> 00:37:54.500
And they're like, really?

769
00:37:54.780 --> 00:37:56.040
Do you like my singing?

770
00:37:56.040 --> 00:37:57.000
Elvis Presley.

771
00:37:57.200 --> 00:37:58.580
You think I'm any good?

772
00:37:58.780 --> 00:37:58.900
Really?

773
00:37:59.380 --> 00:38:03.060
You know, like, you want Elvis Presley to go, yeah, I can sing.

774
00:38:03.920 --> 00:38:05.600
This is the one thing I can do.

775
00:38:05.760 --> 00:38:08.000
Yeah, I mean, you're not a dick.

776
00:38:08.200 --> 00:38:13.440
You're not, you know, you want, I'm not saying I'm anywhere near that level.

777
00:38:13.560 --> 00:38:16.320
But, you know, if you can do something, just calm down.

778
00:38:16.580 --> 00:38:18.360
It's okay to know you can do it.

779
00:38:18.960 --> 00:38:21.260
You know, you're 60 years old now.

780
00:38:21.760 --> 00:38:23.800
There's hundreds of hours of you doing it.

781
00:38:23.960 --> 00:38:24.120
Yeah.

782
00:38:24.120 --> 00:38:30.200
Don't pretend you can't believe that anyone has ever seen you do, because you see that all the time.

783
00:38:30.360 --> 00:38:30.500
Yeah.

784
00:38:31.020 --> 00:38:35.580
Anyway, so I'll admit that I've written a lot of sketches and I'm good at that.

785
00:38:35.580 --> 00:38:46.480
And that I can do drama, which is a bit of a surprise to me, but I also do think it marries up with the kind of energy that I, that we all have what we have.

786
00:38:46.780 --> 00:38:54.460
You know, we also are used to seeing people who wish they were different, wish they had a different energy.

787
00:38:54.460 --> 00:38:58.500
You know, a drama person who wishes they could do comedy.

788
00:38:58.840 --> 00:39:01.780
And, you know, you want to go, well, I don't know.

789
00:39:02.100 --> 00:39:02.480
No.

790
00:39:03.180 --> 00:39:03.380
Yeah.

791
00:39:03.600 --> 00:39:03.700
Yeah.

792
00:39:03.960 --> 00:39:04.740
Just calm down.

793
00:39:04.880 --> 00:39:05.280
It's okay.

794
00:39:05.560 --> 00:39:07.380
I mean, be thankful for what you have.

795
00:39:07.400 --> 00:39:08.340
For what you can do.

796
00:39:08.480 --> 00:39:11.200
I didn't finish my thought from the beginning, which is Brother Solomon.

797
00:39:11.200 --> 00:39:11.940
Your greatest regret?

798
00:39:12.380 --> 00:39:12.640
Yeah.

799
00:39:12.640 --> 00:39:13.340
Oh, yeah.

800
00:39:13.660 --> 00:39:15.560
Look, it is the greatest script.

801
00:39:15.640 --> 00:39:17.540
And I love everybody involved.

802
00:39:17.760 --> 00:39:19.100
What if we had done that?

803
00:39:19.180 --> 00:39:27.200
What if I had engineered it such, not that I could have, I'm not sure I could have, where we shot it like an indie movie.

804
00:39:27.420 --> 00:39:29.320
Put them, don't build sets.

805
00:39:29.560 --> 00:39:29.940
Mm-hmm.

806
00:39:30.440 --> 00:39:32.400
Really work with the real world.

807
00:39:32.620 --> 00:39:37.380
Put those two characters in the real world as much as possible.

808
00:39:37.520 --> 00:39:37.940
Mm-hmm.

809
00:39:38.120 --> 00:39:39.800
I think it might have worked.

810
00:39:40.080 --> 00:39:41.060
Like, really worked.

811
00:39:42.040 --> 00:39:44.300
Many people come up and say they enjoy that movie.

812
00:39:44.540 --> 00:39:45.260
It's so funny.

813
00:39:45.360 --> 00:39:46.100
It's so funny.

814
00:39:46.300 --> 00:39:47.060
It's interesting.

815
00:39:47.320 --> 00:39:48.020
This is like.

816
00:39:48.120 --> 00:39:49.480
They're just, I think it would be better.

817
00:39:49.660 --> 00:39:50.900
I think it would really work.

818
00:39:51.020 --> 00:39:55.040
Like, it would really be like, I know why they made that movie.

819
00:39:55.380 --> 00:39:56.860
That has a purpose.

820
00:39:57.160 --> 00:40:09.380
And to take a very lighthearted conceptual behavior comedy and sink it into a world that feels incredibly real, because it is.

821
00:40:09.540 --> 00:40:09.880
Mm-hmm.

822
00:40:09.880 --> 00:40:12.220
Would be a neat thing.

823
00:40:12.700 --> 00:40:13.500
I don't know.

824
00:40:13.840 --> 00:40:16.580
Well, do you find this with certain projects?

825
00:40:16.580 --> 00:40:21.400
And I think it might be especially true of directors, because I've talked to other directors about this before.

826
00:40:21.400 --> 00:40:31.200
And Lee, my husband, when he directs something, he always has this happen, which is that when you finish the project, you're a better artist than you were when you started it.

827
00:40:31.200 --> 00:40:39.780
And you can only, like, you are better than you were when you started that thing because that thing helped you grow.

828
00:40:39.780 --> 00:40:46.920
And so, if you did it again, it would be better, probably, because you're better.

829
00:40:47.540 --> 00:40:49.560
Like, you had, but you had to do it.

830
00:40:49.700 --> 00:40:49.860
Yeah.

831
00:40:50.040 --> 00:40:51.220
To learn the thing.

832
00:40:51.640 --> 00:40:51.780
Right?

833
00:40:52.580 --> 00:40:56.100
I think with directing in particular, you can always look back.

834
00:40:56.260 --> 00:41:02.280
I mean, unless you're just like Steven Spielberg or something, I guess, and you just nail it on the landing the first time, right?

835
00:41:02.560 --> 00:41:03.900
I think you're probably right.

836
00:41:04.040 --> 00:41:04.240
Yes.

837
00:41:04.440 --> 00:41:05.080
I think you're right.

838
00:41:05.080 --> 00:41:05.260
You're there.

839
00:41:05.460 --> 00:41:05.720
Yeah.

840
00:41:05.940 --> 00:41:07.320
Like an artist's journey.

841
00:41:07.640 --> 00:41:09.900
Yeah, like, you couldn't know what you didn't know.

842
00:41:10.040 --> 00:41:10.200
Right.

843
00:41:10.360 --> 00:41:16.660
You know, it's like, there's what you know, there's what you don't know, and then there's what you don't know that you don't know.

844
00:41:16.700 --> 00:41:17.000
Yes.

845
00:41:17.000 --> 00:41:19.160
And you can't know that until you know it.

846
00:41:19.180 --> 00:41:31.400
This is, like, the thing about showbiz, and there's luck involved, and there's so many elements involved, and, you know, I think about older actors and how, in a weird way,

847
00:41:31.460 --> 00:41:32.340
they may have learned.

848
00:41:32.340 --> 00:41:42.740
And there's a lesson in this, in what we're talking about, too, where I sometimes feel that they're extremely aware that they've done projects where they worked so hard,

849
00:41:42.860 --> 00:41:47.500
and they worked so earnestly, and everybody did their best, and it didn't play well.

850
00:41:47.500 --> 00:41:48.240
It didn't work.

851
00:41:48.400 --> 00:41:56.380
And then they've done projects where people showed up a little bit half-assed, wasn't figured out, they were scared, and it was great.

852
00:41:56.420 --> 00:41:57.680
It really worked.

853
00:41:57.680 --> 00:42:03.120
And I think one thing people come away with after that is, it's not worth trying.

854
00:42:03.620 --> 00:42:05.000
Just make the damn thing.

855
00:42:05.220 --> 00:42:06.560
It might work, it might not.

856
00:42:06.680 --> 00:42:07.700
Who the hell knows?

857
00:42:07.860 --> 00:42:08.200
Mm-hmm.

858
00:42:08.360 --> 00:42:10.860
It's not worth sweating it too much.

859
00:42:11.040 --> 00:42:11.800
It's not so precious.

860
00:42:12.280 --> 00:42:12.680
Yeah, yeah.

861
00:42:12.760 --> 00:42:20.320
Young actors and writers are, like, every second of the day trying to manipulate every little corner of that project to make it great.

862
00:42:21.860 --> 00:42:25.300
And the truth lies somewhere in between these two, you know?

863
00:42:25.460 --> 00:42:25.600
Yeah.

864
00:42:25.980 --> 00:42:30.640
The other thing is, so many people want to be directors, you know?

865
00:42:30.820 --> 00:42:36.000
And I encourage everybody to do and make everything, you know, just do stuff.

866
00:42:36.280 --> 00:42:39.600
And that's what, you know, make stuff today, every day.

867
00:42:39.860 --> 00:42:40.880
Write a sentence.

868
00:42:41.140 --> 00:42:41.760
Write a poem.

869
00:42:42.400 --> 00:42:43.780
Write a journal entry.

870
00:42:44.220 --> 00:42:44.980
Do a drawing.

871
00:42:45.280 --> 00:42:45.920
Every day.

872
00:42:46.080 --> 00:42:49.000
I hate to put the kibosh on anyone's dreams at all.

873
00:42:49.000 --> 00:42:50.200
I encourage people.

874
00:42:50.320 --> 00:42:54.800
But when you meet somebody like Spielberg, who's just a director, you know?

875
00:42:54.920 --> 00:42:55.100
Yeah.

876
00:42:55.140 --> 00:42:56.960
He's born for this.

877
00:42:57.420 --> 00:43:03.260
You do go, like, oh, well, yeah, I could direct something, but he's a director.

878
00:43:03.820 --> 00:43:04.040
Yeah.

879
00:43:04.520 --> 00:43:07.020
I don't want to be a director or a writer.

880
00:43:07.060 --> 00:43:08.640
I hate both of those things.

881
00:43:08.920 --> 00:43:09.240
Yeah.

882
00:43:09.240 --> 00:43:11.660
I'm starting to think I hate writing a little bit.

883
00:43:11.760 --> 00:43:12.120
What?

884
00:43:13.260 --> 00:43:14.120
I don't know.

885
00:43:14.340 --> 00:43:15.240
I'm 61.

886
00:43:15.500 --> 00:43:17.580
Shouldn't you get more patient as you get older?

887
00:43:17.580 --> 00:43:23.820
I'm working on a couple movie scripts, and I'm stalled out in a couple different ways.

888
00:43:23.960 --> 00:43:26.280
And it really is making me angry.

889
00:43:26.560 --> 00:43:27.680
Like, what the f***?

890
00:43:27.960 --> 00:43:28.620
If you, how?

891
00:43:29.260 --> 00:43:30.100
Come on.

892
00:43:30.320 --> 00:43:30.640
Really?

893
00:43:30.920 --> 00:43:31.260
Still?

894
00:43:32.020 --> 00:43:32.880
But yeah.

895
00:43:33.100 --> 00:43:33.400
Yeah.

896
00:43:33.620 --> 00:43:35.360
I mean, it's just the hardest f***.

897
00:43:36.200 --> 00:43:38.700
Some people love writing, and I'm like, really?

898
00:43:38.900 --> 00:43:40.240
You love writing?

899
00:43:40.380 --> 00:43:44.680
You love looking at a completely blank page and having to put something on it?

900
00:43:44.680 --> 00:43:45.560
By the way, and then—

901
00:43:45.560 --> 00:43:46.580
To me, that's just, like, the worst ever.

902
00:43:46.580 --> 00:43:50.920
That's my favorite part, because I pace, and I get ideas, and I just throw it all down,

903
00:43:51.040 --> 00:43:51.820
and I'm excited.

904
00:43:52.300 --> 00:43:53.600
But then my structure sucks.

905
00:43:53.680 --> 00:43:54.900
But that's what you're good at, lady.

906
00:43:55.100 --> 00:43:56.060
I love to edit.

907
00:43:56.360 --> 00:43:58.120
I'll edit till the cows come home.

908
00:43:58.220 --> 00:44:01.800
Give me something to chew on, and I can make it great.

909
00:44:01.880 --> 00:44:04.760
But I am not putting any ink on a blank page.

910
00:44:05.180 --> 00:44:06.480
I don't have anything for that.

911
00:44:06.700 --> 00:44:11.640
We wrote a book together, and this was, like, I think the way our personalities complemented

912
00:44:11.640 --> 00:44:12.020
each other.

913
00:44:12.140 --> 00:44:14.780
But when I told Jenna, I was like, Jenna, I want us to write a book together.

914
00:44:15.380 --> 00:44:17.760
I want it to be a New York Times bestseller, and we're going to do it.

915
00:44:17.820 --> 00:44:19.600
And she was like, Angela, I need you to hear me.

916
00:44:19.800 --> 00:44:22.060
I am never writing another book again.

917
00:44:22.240 --> 00:44:23.460
I wrote one book by myself.

918
00:44:23.560 --> 00:44:24.540
Yeah, you wrote a great book.

919
00:44:24.680 --> 00:44:24.860
Yeah.

920
00:44:24.960 --> 00:44:25.380
Thank you.

921
00:44:25.560 --> 00:44:25.820
Yeah.

922
00:44:26.000 --> 00:44:27.820
Well, I should give Lee credit for a lot of that.

923
00:44:27.820 --> 00:44:28.800
Say the name of your book.

924
00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:30.760
What is it?

925
00:44:31.960 --> 00:44:34.380
It's an actor's life.

926
00:44:34.700 --> 00:44:37.000
It's called The Actor's Life, A Survival Guide.

927
00:44:37.280 --> 00:44:38.300
It's really great.

928
00:44:38.480 --> 00:44:39.300
It is fantastic.

929
00:44:39.320 --> 00:44:42.200
And then we wrote another one together, and so you did do it.

930
00:44:42.260 --> 00:44:43.820
So you are a writer, and you're a good writer.

931
00:44:43.960 --> 00:44:45.400
I don't enjoy it.

932
00:44:46.180 --> 00:44:49.140
Yeah, I'm talking about people who claim to love writing.

933
00:44:49.560 --> 00:44:50.900
It's so much work.

934
00:44:51.080 --> 00:44:52.020
It's such a slog.

935
00:44:52.080 --> 00:44:58.300
By the way, I get, I love the feeling of reward from having written.

936
00:44:58.300 --> 00:45:02.720
And when it turns out, and I know I'm proud of it, I get that.

937
00:45:02.880 --> 00:45:03.060
Yeah.

938
00:45:03.140 --> 00:45:03.440
Of course.

939
00:45:03.640 --> 00:45:03.720
Yeah.

940
00:45:03.880 --> 00:45:04.560
Good for you.

941
00:45:04.800 --> 00:45:05.080
Yes.

942
00:45:05.780 --> 00:45:11.560
But getting to that point, I mean, don't tell me you do that every day when you write.

943
00:45:11.940 --> 00:45:12.280
Mm-mm.

944
00:45:12.380 --> 00:45:13.500
Because no way.

945
00:45:13.760 --> 00:45:13.940
No.

946
00:45:14.540 --> 00:45:19.580
But I do think there are people like Stephen King must be-

947
00:45:19.580 --> 00:45:20.540
He must like writing.

948
00:45:20.760 --> 00:45:21.700
Having the time of his life.

949
00:45:21.720 --> 00:45:23.180
He has a bowl of popcorn.

950
00:45:23.540 --> 00:45:26.760
He has, like, cold water.

951
00:45:26.900 --> 00:45:27.860
Sodas ready to go.

952
00:45:27.860 --> 00:45:28.340
Sodas.

953
00:45:28.460 --> 00:45:29.320
He's enjoying it.

954
00:45:29.400 --> 00:45:29.540
Yeah, yeah.

955
00:45:29.540 --> 00:45:32.180
He's, like, eating and typing and laughing.

956
00:45:32.620 --> 00:45:32.820
Snacks.

957
00:45:33.560 --> 00:45:36.600
This is how we imagine Stephen King's writing day.

958
00:45:36.720 --> 00:45:38.260
He's giggling and laughing.

959
00:45:38.260 --> 00:45:39.280
He loves it.

960
00:45:39.380 --> 00:45:40.480
He's such a laugher.

961
00:45:40.900 --> 00:45:42.440
And going, oh, what?

962
00:45:42.540 --> 00:45:43.240
No way!

963
00:45:43.500 --> 00:45:44.520
And then typing more.

964
00:45:46.740 --> 00:45:50.800
You know, please tell me that's what he's done with his life.

965
00:45:50.800 --> 00:45:57.260
If he was dragging his ass through it like I do, then I want to say to him, please stop.

966
00:45:57.700 --> 00:45:57.750
Pivot.

967
00:45:57.940 --> 00:45:58.280
Pivot.

968
00:45:58.680 --> 00:45:59.060
I definitely-

969
00:45:59.060 --> 00:46:04.160
With the rest of the time you have left to breathe and see and do things, don't torture

970
00:46:04.160 --> 00:46:05.500
yourself anymore.

971
00:46:05.880 --> 00:46:08.820
The only part of that scenario that I do is the eating.

972
00:46:09.020 --> 00:46:09.580
You do?

973
00:46:09.580 --> 00:46:10.280
You do the popcorn?

974
00:46:10.300 --> 00:46:11.260
I do a ton-

975
00:46:11.260 --> 00:46:12.820
Bowls and bowls of carbs.

976
00:46:12.840 --> 00:46:14.380
You ate a whole box of-

977
00:46:14.380 --> 00:46:16.380
Was it, like, Cheez-Its or something one time?

978
00:46:16.380 --> 00:46:16.720
No.

979
00:46:16.860 --> 00:46:18.920
Trader Joe's has these tiny-

980
00:46:18.920 --> 00:46:20.260
Oh, it's like a cracker.

981
00:46:20.260 --> 00:46:21.120
Sandwich crackers.

982
00:46:21.320 --> 00:46:22.740
Mini cheese sandwich crackers.

983
00:46:23.040 --> 00:46:25.660
And I can eat them at the box full.

984
00:46:25.740 --> 00:46:26.460
Are they good, huh?

985
00:46:26.620 --> 00:46:27.220
They're terrific.

986
00:46:27.500 --> 00:46:28.060
I'll just sit there-

987
00:46:28.060 --> 00:46:29.520
Are they like the little round ones?

988
00:46:29.540 --> 00:46:30.260
Little round ones.

989
00:46:30.360 --> 00:46:31.560
Oh, I think we have some.

990
00:46:31.700 --> 00:46:32.120
They're great.

991
00:46:32.120 --> 00:46:32.900
My son bought them.

992
00:46:33.000 --> 00:46:33.420
They're great.

993
00:46:33.700 --> 00:46:34.120
My son-

994
00:46:34.120 --> 00:46:34.700
Don't eat one.

995
